From 9a32a191911d56b66b8bedc80ff165079665e10b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: d-b-c-e <anthony.mason1@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 4 Sep 2026 21:20:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 82/88] FFB conditioning moves to the shared toolkit shaper

mvffb's hand-written chain (impulse bypass, dt-aware low-pass, settle-to-zero,
re-level threshold) is now dbce::force::Shaper driven by a named profile, so a
feel can be swapped by editing text instead of rebuilding the emulator.
MIDV_FFB_PROFILE selects it; cruisn-vunit@1 is the default and reproduces
exactly what this code was doing.

Cruis'n is the toolkit's first SHAPER-ONLY consumer. The arcade board hands
over a finished motor byte, so there is no physics and Model is never built.

The feel is measured, not assumed: the toolkit's CruisnVectorTests transcribes
this file's previous algorithm verbatim and drives both it and cruisn-vunit@1
with 62 s of real motor bytes captured on the rig, at the worker's 4 ms
cadence. Worst difference 33 of 32767, mean 1.8 - one re-level step, which is
as close as two implementations get when they differ only in where rounding
happens.

midv_ffb_write now stores the UN-GAINED normalised level. The shaper owns the
gain (shaper.strength), and applying it in both places would square it - the
launcher's FFB STRENGTH is a plain percent while shaper.strength is
50-is-unity, so it is halved on the way in.

If force-profiles.ini is missing, built-in values identical to cruisn-vunit@1
are used and the log says so. Force feedback does not depend on a data file
being present.

Headers under src/mame/midway/dbce/ are VENDORED copies pinned at v0.9.0, not
sources. See dbce/README.md, including the note that compiling them in makes
the combined binary GPL-3.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
